  [1mB. Identity module, plus [38;5;153muserId[39m as an optional third input[0m
  Everything in A, and [38;5;153mlogin(username, password, userId)[39m sends the stored ID (or [38;5;153mnull[39m on first visit) so the server can
  link a returning device to an account. The response remains authoritative and overwrites whatever was sent.
  [3mBuys:[0m the literal request; return-visit correlation. [3mCosts:[0m a third positional boolean-ish trap parameter, and a
  client-controlled value the backend contract must explicitly mark untrusted. [3mRules out:[0m nothing, but every future
  field makes the positional list worse.

  [1mC. Options-object signature[0m
  Identity module as in A, but [38;5;153mlogin({ username, password, userId })[39m.
  [3mBuys:[0m the signature stops growing as fields accumulate; call sites are self-documenting. [3mCosts:[0m changes the existing
  call shape at [38;5;153mapp.js:23[39m for a function with one caller — ceremony this codebase hasn't earned yet. [3mRules out:[0m nothing.

  I recommend [1mA[0m, and B only if you actually want return-visit correlation — I'd rather not add a parameter whose only
  justification is the original wording. Pick B and I'll spec the untrusted-input rule explicitly.

  Two smaller things I'll fold into the design once you pick:

  - [1mModule loading.[0m [38;5;153mapp.js[39m uses plain browser globals; [38;5;153msrc/[39m uses CommonJS and isn't loaded by the page. I'd introduce
    [38;5;153midentity.js[39m as an ES module and switch [38;5;153mindex.html[39m to [38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m, rather than adding a third convention.
  - [1mTooling.[0m This repo has no linter, formatter, or test runner, and [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m has no scripts. Storage code with a
    persisted format is exactly the kind of thing worth testing. Cheapest to set up now, before code exists.
